{
  "text": "```systemverilog\nmodule rstgen (\n    input  logic clk_i,\n    input  logic rst_ni,\n    input  logic test_mode_i,\n    output logic rst_no,\n    output logic init_no\n);\n\n    // Synchronize the asynchronous reset signal to the clk_i domain\n    // Using a 2-stage synchronizer to prevent metastability\n    logic rst_n_sync_1;\n    logic rst_n_sync_2;\n\n    always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_ni) begin\n        if (!rst_ni) begin\n            rst_n_sync_1 <= 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            rst_n_sync_1 <= rst_n_sync_1;\n        end\n    end\n\n    always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_n_sync_1) begin\n        if (!rst_n_sync_1) begin\n            rst_n_sync_2 <= 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            rst_n_sync_2 <= rst_n_sync_2;\n        end\n    end\n\n    // Generate the synchronized reset\n    // If test_mode_i is high, bypass the synchronizer and use the raw input directly\n    // This allows for faster reset release during testing if needed, though care must be taken\n    assign rst_no = test_mode_i ? rst_ni : rst_n_sync_2;\n\n    // Generate the initialization indication\n    // The module is considered initialized when the synchronized reset is released\n    // and the system has been stable for at least one clock cycle after reset release.\n    // We use the inverted synchronized reset to indicate \"not reset\".\n    // init_no is active low, meaning it is 0 when initialized.\n    // It stays high (1) during reset and for one cycle after reset release to ensure stability.\n    // Logic: If rst_n_sync_2 is high (reset released), we wait one cycle before asserting init_no.\n    // However, a simpler and more robust interpretation for \"initialization indication\" \n    // in this context is often: \"Are we currently in the reset state or the first cycle after reset?\"\n    // Let's define init_no as active-low. It should be 0 when the system is fully initialized.\n    // It should be 1 during reset and potentially for one cycle after reset to allow state machines to settle.\n    \n    // Standard approach: \n    // 1. During reset (rst_n_sync_2 == 0), init_no = 1.\n    // 2. One cycle after reset releases, init_no = 1 (to allow logic to settle).\n    // 3. From the second cycle after reset releases, init_no = 0.\n    \n    logic rst_n_sync_3;\n    \n    always_ff @(posedge clk_i or negedge rst_n_sync_2) begin\n        if (!rst_n_sync_2) begin\n            rst_n_sync_3 <= 1'b1;\n        end else begin\n            rst_n_sync_3 <= rst_n_sync_3;\n        end\n    end\n\n    // init_no is active low. \n    // It is 1 (inactive) during reset and for one cycle after reset release.\n    // It becomes 0 (active) once the system has settled for two cycles after reset release.\n    assign init_no = (rst_n_sync_2 || rst_n_sync_3) ? 1'b1 : 1'b0;\n\nendmodule\n```<|im_end|>\n<|endoftext|>",
